.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH qsh 1  "qsh 0.6.0" 
.SH NAME
qsh \- QSH: a QUIC\-based direct\-connect remote shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBqsh\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-jsonl\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]... [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-ssh\fR] [\fB\-\-install\-remote\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-install\-remote\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-punch\fR] [\fB\-\-rendezvous\fR] [\fB\-\-jump\fR] [\fB\-e\fR|\fB\-\-escape\-char\fR] [\fB\-\-screen\-sync\fR] [\fB\-\-predict\fR] [\fB\-\-status\-line\fR] [\fB\-l \fR] [\fB\-p \fR] [\fB\-i \fR] [\fB\-F \fR] [\fB\-o \fR] [\fB\-C \fR] [\fB\-4 \fR] [\fB\-6 \fR] [\fB\-J \fR] [\fB\-A \fR] [\fB\-a \fR] [\fB\-t \fR]... [\fB\-T \fR] [\fB\-N \fR] [\fB\-f \fR] [\fB\-E \fR] [\fB\-L \fR] [\fB\-R \fR] [\fB\-D \fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fI[USER@]HOST\fR] [\fIREMOTE_COMMAND\fR] [\fIsubcommands\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
QSH: a QUIC\-based direct\-connect remote shell
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit a single `qsh.cli/v1` JSON envelope on stdout instead of human\-readable text
.TP
\fB\-\-jsonl\fR
Emit newline\-delimited JSON on stdout (reserved for streaming commands; non\-streaming commands emit a single line, same as `\-\-json`)
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Increase diagnostic verbosity on stderr (`\-v` info, `\-vv` debug, `\-vvv` trace). Never affects stdout (`docs/CLI.md` §2.2)
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress diagnostic output on stderr
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-ssh\fR
Never run ssh on this command\*(Aqs behalf: no automatic bootstrap of an unknown name, and no restarting of a pinned host\*(Aqs serve over ssh when the connection fails (`docs/CLI.md` §7). `qsh pair ssh` is not affected; it is ssh by definition
.TP
\fB\-\-install\-remote\fR
When the ssh bootstrap finds no `qsh` on the remote host (or one too old to answer it), install this machine\*(Aqs release into `~/.local/bin` there without asking (`docs/CLI.md` §7). Works with `\-\-json`
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-install\-remote\fR
Never install or upgrade `qsh` on the remote host, and do not ask; a missing `qsh` ends as `remote_qsh_missing`
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-punch\fR
Never start a hole punch on this command\*(Aqs behalf, and send no `PunchRequest` (`docs/CLI.md` §7, ADR\-0054 decision 19). The route is the direct dial alone. Wins over `[punch].mode`
.TP
\fB\-\-rendezvous\fR \fI<PEER>\fR
Use this pinned peer as the rendezvous for hole punching on this command, instead of the host\*(Aqs own `rendezvous` hints (`docs/CLI.md` §7, ADR\-0054 decision 4). Must be pinned with an address. Not stored
.TP
\fB\-\-jump\fR \fI<HOPS>\fR
Reach the host through these pinned peers, first hop first, for this command: `a` or `a,b` (`docs/CLI.md` §7, ADR\-0056 decision 20). Each hop is the name of a pin; a `user@` or `:port` on it is ignored by the QUIC path. `none` goes the ordinary way even when the host\*(Aqs pin has a `jump`. Wins over `\-o ProxyJump` and over the pin\*(Aqs own `jump`. Not stored. The short `\-J` is the same flag on the commands that take it
.TP
\fB\-e\fR, \fB\-\-escape\-char\fR \fI<CHAR>\fR
Escape character for the line\-start sequences (`~.` ends the session, `~d` detaches, `~^Z`, `~#`, `~~`, `~?`), or `none` to disable them. Default `~`; only active when stdin is a terminal. `\-e` is the ssh spelling
.TP
\fB\-\-screen\-sync\fR \fI<MODE>\fR
Screen state sync for the interactive session (ADR\-0052): `auto` (default) uses it when the host offers it and the terminal is a terminal on both ends, `on` requires it (`UNSUPPORTED` otherwise), `off` keeps the plain byte stream. Interactive sessions only
.TP
\fB\-\-predict\fR \fI<MODE>\fR
Local echo prediction (ADR\-0053): `auto` (default) follows the adaptive policy, `always` draws regardless of RTT, `never` turns it off. Interactive sessions on the screen path only (host must advertise `screen\-echo.v1`)
.TP
\fB\-\-status\-line\fR \fI<MODE>\fR
Link\-status line (ADR\-0053): `on` (default) or `off`. Interactive sessions on the screen path only
.TP
\fB\-l\fR \fI<USER>\fR
Remote account, `ssh \-l`. Same as `user@` on the host
.TP
\fB\-p\fR \fI<PORT>\fR
ssh port, `ssh \-p`. It is sshd\*(Aqs port for the bootstrap, never the UDP port qsh dials (ADR\-0051 decision 7)
.TP
\fB\-i\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
ssh identity file, `ssh \-i`, repeatable. Passed to the bootstrap ssh
.TP
\fB\-F\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
ssh config file, `ssh \-F`. Passed to the bootstrap ssh
.TP
\fB\-o\fR \fI<KEY=VALUE>\fR
ssh option `Key=Value`, `ssh \-o`, repeatable. Passed to the bootstrap ssh; `ConnectTimeout`, `BatchMode`, `ServerAliveInterval` and `ServerAliveCountMax` also apply to the QUIC connection
.TP
\fB\-C\fR
Compression for the bootstrap ssh, `ssh \-C`
.TP
\fB\-4\fR
IPv4 only: the bootstrap ssh and the QUIC dial
.TP
\fB\-6\fR
IPv6 only: the bootstrap ssh and the QUIC dial
.TP
\fB\-J\fR \fI<HOPS>\fR
Jump host list, `ssh \-J`, as pin names (ADR\-0056): the QUIC connection goes through these pinned peers, and the bootstrap ssh goes through the same list. `none` skips the pin\*(Aqs own `jump`
.TP
\fB\-A\fR
Forward the local ssh\-agent to the host for this command, `ssh \-A` (ADR\-0055). Only listing keys and signing reach your agent. With `\-a`, the last one given wins
.TP
\fB\-a\fR
Do not forward the agent for this command, `ssh \-a`
.TP
\fB\-t\fR
Force a PTY (`\-t`); twice (`\-tt`) force it even when stdin is not a terminal. With a command, `\-t` runs it in a PTY session that can be detached
.TP
\fB\-T\fR
No PTY. With no command, opens the login shell without one
.TP
\fB\-N\fR
Run no command and no shell: hold only the `\-L`/`\-R`/`\-D` forwards, like `qsh tunnel open`
.TP
\fB\-f\fR
Go to the background once authentication and the forwards are up (with `\-N`)
.TP
\fB\-E\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Write diagnostics to this file instead of stderr, `ssh \-E`
.TP
\fB\-L\fR \fI<SPEC>\fR
Local forward `[bind:]listen_port:host:host_port`, repeatable: open `listen_port` on this machine and forward each connection to `host:host_port` as seen from the peer. `bind` defaults to (and is restricted to) loopback.

The listener lives exactly as long as this interactive session and dies with the process (`docs/CLI.md` §6.14) — there is no daemon and nothing to close.

Deliberately **not** parsed by a clap `value_parser`: a malformed spec is an `INVALID_ARGUMENT` operation error (exit `255`, with a `qsh.cli/v1` envelope in machine mode), not a clap usage error (exit `2`), and `qsh_core::parse_local_forwards` is the single place that decides which code a spec earns (`docs/CLI.md` §6.9).
.TP
\fB\-R\fR \fI<SPEC>\fR
Remote forward `[bind:]rport:host:hport`, repeatable: ask the peer to bind `rport` (loopback\-only — `docs/PRD.md` §9) and forward each connection it accepts back to `host:hport` **on this machine** (`docs/CLI.md` §6.9). The two legs are swapped relative to `\-L`; see [`Self::local_forward`]\*(Aqs own doc for the lifecycle this shares with it — same holder, same teardown, no daemon.

Same reasoning as [`Self::local_forward`] for not being a clap `value_parser`: `qsh_core::parse_remote_forwards` decides the `docs/CLI.md` §3.3 code, not clap\*(Aqs usage\-error path.
.TP
\fB\-D\fR \fI<SPEC>\fR
SOCKS5 dynamic forwarding `[bind:]port`, repeatable: open a SOCKS5 proxy on this machine and, for every CONNECT a SOCKS client sends it, forward to whatever destination that CONNECT names, as seen from the peer (ADR\-0019). `bind` defaults to (and is restricted to) loopback, same as `\-L`/`\-R`.

Every listener this flag opens lives exactly as long as this interactive session and dies with the process, same lifecycle as `\-L` (`docs/CLI.md` §6.14) — there is no daemon and nothing to close.

There is no ACL check at open time: each CONNECT is authorized on the peer as `forward.local`, the same action `\-L` uses. `DYNAMIC_FORWARD_ACL_NOTE` — "`\-D` runs SOCKS5 on this machine and authorizes every CONNECT on the peer as `forward.local`; `forward.socks` is never consulted." Works over both forward and reverse routes (ADR\-0020 decisions 1–3) — a reverse route relays each CONNECT through this machine\*(Aqs resident `qsh listen` daemon, with the same host\-local address filter a forward route enforces. The one refusal left is `UNSUPPORTED` before anything binds when the connected route never negotiated `dial\-filter.v1`; on a reverse route the message names both possible causes (the target\*(Aqs qsh may predate the capability, or this machine\*(Aqs own `qsh listen` daemon may have started before this machine\*(Aqs own upgrade) (`docs/CLI.md` §6.9).

With `\-\-json`/`\-\-jsonl` also given, §7\*(Aqs machine\-mode gate answers `INVALID_ARGUMENT` before this flag is looked at at all — the interactive form has no JSON output mode of its own.

Not shape\-checked here for the same reason as [`Self::local_forward`]/[`Self::remote_forward`]: `qsh_core::parse_dynamic_forwards` is the single place that decides which `docs/CLI.md` §3.3 code a malformed spec earns.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.TP
[\fI[USER@]HOST\fR]
Pinned host to open an interactive session on, optionally prefixed with the expected remote login name (`dave@personal\-mac`)
.TP
[\fIREMOTE_COMMAND\fR]
The command to run on the host, `ssh host command [arg...]`: the arguments are joined with spaces and run by the remote account\*(Aqs shell (`docs/CLI.md` §7.2, ADR\-0051 decision 6). Everything after the host is the command, options included. Without a command the form opens an interactive session
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
qsh\-version(1)
Print qsh\*(Aqs version and the wire/CLI schemas it understands
.TP
qsh\-schema(1)
Print the JSON Schema of the `qsh.cli/v1` envelope and every command\*(Aqs `data` payload (`docs/CLI.md` §6.10) — the same schemas golden fixtures are validated against
.TP
qsh\-capabilities(1)
Print this build\*(Aqs supported capabilities, or (with a host) the capabilities actually negotiated with that pinned peer (`docs/CLI.md` §6.10)
.TP
qsh\-doctor(1)
Diagnose this deployment: identity, ACL policy, audit log, trust store, clock and (best\-effort) network reachability (`docs/CLI.md` §6.17). Exits `0` on a successful run — findings are reported as data, not exit status (`overall` field) — unless `\-\-fail\-on` is given (ADR\-0027); only doctor\*(Aqs own inability to run at all (e.g. no identity yet) fails the command
.TP
qsh\-init(1)
Create this device\*(Aqs identity (keypair + self\-signed certificate). Idempotent: re\-running reports the existing identity
.TP
qsh\-identity(1)
Manage this device\*(Aqs own identity (`docs/CLI.md` §6.11)
.TP
qsh\-trust(1)
Manage the trust store (pinned peers)
.TP
qsh\-pair(1)
Pair with another device: mint an invite on one side, redeem it on the other (ADR\-0002, ADR\-0012 decision 3, `docs/CLI.md` §6.11). This is the documented spelling for creating a new trust entry by pairing; `trust …` stays the vocabulary for manipulating entries that already exist
.TP
qsh\-config(1)
Read and edit this machine\*(Aqs `config.toml` by dotted key (`docs/CLI.md` §6.23, ADR\-0051 decision 10)
.TP
qsh\-cert(1)
Manage the private CA (`docs/adr/0008\-private\-ca\-cert\-issuance.md`)
.TP
qsh\-acl(1)
Inspect the ACL policy (`docs/CLI.md` §6.15)
.TP
qsh\-audit(1)
Read this machine\*(Aqs own audit log: the authorization decisions `qsh serve`, `qsh listen` and `qsh serve \-\-to` recorded, newest first, filtered on this machine (`docs/CLI.md` §6.22)
.TP
qsh\-setup(1)
Set this machine up for one role, step by step, using only the commands you could run yourself (ADR\-0024, `docs/CLI.md` §6.20). Prints the `[[acl]]` rows to add and never writes `acl.toml`; never starts `qsh serve`, `qsh listen` or a session
.TP
qsh\-hosts(1)
List every host visible to this machine: trust\-store\-pinned forward hosts and this machine\*(Aqs live reverse registrations, together (`docs/CLI.md` §6.1). Never dials
.TP
qsh\-host(1)
Look up one host
.TP
qsh\-exec(1)
Run a command on a pinned host and return its output
.TP
qsh\-cp(1)
Copy files between this machine and one qsh host, scp\-style (`docs/CLI.md` §6.21)
.TP
qsh\-attach(1)
Re\-attach this terminal to a session that is already running
.TP
qsh\-session(1)
Manage sessions: shells that outlive the connection that opened them
.TP
qsh\-sessions(1)
List sessions on one pinned host, or on every pinned host
.TP
qsh\-serve(1)
Run the host: accept connections from pinned peers, or (with `\-\-to`) dial out and register as a reverse target instead (`docs/CLI.md` §6.12/§6.13, ADR\-0012 decision 2/4). Foreground only; the bound address, or the dial/registration events, go to stderr
.TP
qsh\-listen(1)
Run the reverse\-mode controller: accept dial\-in registrations from `qsh serve \-\-to` (the hidden `qsh reverse` alias dials the same way) and serve them as hosts (`docs/CLI.md` §6.13). Foreground only; the bound address and registration events go to stderr
.TP
qsh\-service(1)
Manage this machine\*(Aqs platform service unit for its inferred run mode (`docs/CLI.md` §6.18): `install`/`uninstall`/`status` against launchd (macOS) or systemd (Linux) user units. `UNSUPPORTED` on every other platform
.TP
qsh\-tunnel(1)
Manage tunnels (`docs/CLI.md` §6.9)
.TP
qsh\-tunnels(1)
List every tunnel this machine\*(Aqs resident `qsh listen` daemon(s) currently hold (`docs/CLI.md` §6.9, `tunnel.list`). Never dials. A forward\-route tunnel opened by a standalone `qsh tunnel open` process is not visible here — it has no resident holder to be listed by (`Ops::tunnel_list`\*(Aqs own doc)
.SH VERSION
v0.6.0
